        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>OBJECTIVES:</B>
<BR>The purpose of this study was to 1) develop a new method of quantifying daily activity level in terms of the daily history of ground reaction forces and 2) to clarify the relationship between musculoskeletal tissue stress (strain) histories and the material and structural properties of bone during normal daily activity and 3) to establish equivalent loading histories in space compared to baseline activity levels on Earth.
<P>
<B>APPROACH:</B><BR>
Male subjects, aged 30-55 years of average weight and similar shoe size were selected. An insole sensor (1/8th inch thick) was fitted to a foot of each subject.  Pressure between the foot and shoe sole caused the sensor to deform and output a voltage (2.5 Volts maximum) proportional to the applied force.  A shielding wire threaded through the pant leg connects the insole sensor with a microprocessor unit that is attached to the waist for data acquisition.  The sensor is calibrated to zero and one body weight by lifting the foot off the ground for 2.5 seconds and then a second reading is taken after placing the foot on the ground for 2.5 seconds.  These static data points are stored to obtain a linear force voltage output calibration and compared with a set of dynamic points from a swaying subject to ensure no difference in readings greater than 5% that would indicate a defective sensor.
<P>
<B>RESULTS:</B><BR>
Peak cyclic ground reaction forces (GRFz), normalized to subject body weight were recorded for three consecutive weekdays. Average daily histograms of the number of loading cycles vs cycle-magnitudes (loading histories) were obtained by averaging 24 hour periods.  The total number of cycles for men were 4526 ±1187 and for women, 4744 ±1383.  The total number of cycles greater than 0.8 BW were 3649 ±1139 for men and 3909 ±1259 for women.  Mean cycle magnitude for all cycles greater than 0.8 BW were 1.11 ±0.07 BW for men and 1.13 ±0.09 for women.  The number of cycles at the higher >0.2 BW for men were 7.3±12.5 and for women 5.4±9.5.  A trend toward reduced high load activity was observed with age.   These results demonstrate for the first time objective force measurements of human activity in men and women.  The range in frequency and magnitude of daily loading may be an important contributor to variation in bone density in lower limb skeletal sites loaded down by internal forces supporting the ground reaction force.<END>
        </div>

